## Placement

Keep module entrypoints focused on orchestration.
Make file layout follow recursive code flow.

Keep helpers and algorithms local, with conversions beside their type.

Prefer one data model per file, keeping entrypoint definitions at entrypoints.

Keep enums, newtypes and value objects with their sole parent type.

## Visibility

Keep APIs as private as production callers and contracts allow.

## Body layout

In method bodies:

- Separate coherent groups of steps with one blank line.
- Explain steps/resulting states with short comments where intent is unclear.
- Separate arrange, act and assert with comments in tests.

## Redundancy

Extract helpers for repeated code.
Prefer one parameterizable local helper over per-test mock structs.

## Test strategy

### Coverage

Test all new code.
Delete tests made unnecessary by code removal/change.

Map removed redundant assertions to surviving tests.
Retain test redundancy only across public entry points.

### Parameterization

Parameterize tests where possible.
Prefer named framework cases.

Use a framework such as Rust's rstest.
Add it if needed.

Prefer parameterization over loops of inputs.

### Arguments

Order case arguments: primary input, mode/flags, expected output.

Keep readable cases near 80-100 characters per line.

### Naming and grouping

Name tests `subject_should_expectation_when_condition`.
Use the language's identifier style.

Use `when` only for conditional/edge behavior.
Omit module-redundant prefixes from test names.

Group related tests with lightweight section comments.
Order test groups: construction, core behavior, edge cases, convenience.

## Performance

Prefer the highest-performance correct implementation.
Simplify for readability, never at meaningful performance cost.

Avoid needless allocation, clones, initialization, etc.

## Documentation

Write plain, concise docs for the audience.

### Coverage

Document all new or changed public items.

Use real APIs and hermetic fixtures in examples.
Update docs/comments in place.
Keep unrequested patch history in commits/PRs.

Link existing coverage instead of repeating it.
Document private APIs only if nontrivial.

### Reader understanding

Explain prerequisite concepts and terms before use, without assuming expertise.

Explain how and why complex mechanisms work.
Use short worked examples to clarify mechanisms or relationships.

Add text only for unmet reader needs.
Preserve needed explanations, contracts and caveats.

Use `# Remarks` or equivalent for miscellaneous caveats.

### Error documentation

List every public API error with its cause and a concise explanation.

